(* ::Package:: *)
(* TwoLoopSinc.wl -- general terms of the Sinc sums for the two-loop electron g-2 graphs, in three forms:

     "All"      all D = 6 Schwinger parameters summed.  The integrand is the Schwinger (exponential) form
                S(x) = Sum_blocks (+/-) Sum_n T_n(x) Exp[-V(x)]/Gamma[n],
                obtained from the projective kernel by  1/V^n -> Exp[-V]/Gamma[n]  in every forest block.
     "Delta"    Cheng-Wu: the integral over the overall scale is done exactly, leaving  1/V^n,  and the
                delta function  delta(1 - x4)  removes the sum over the photon x4.  D = 5.
     "DeltaGL"  as "Delta", and in addition the two electron lines at the external vertex are written as
                sigma u, sigma (1-u), and the split u is integrated exactly by a single node at the weighted
                mean of u (the integrand times the simplex weight is linear in u), so only sigma is summed.
                Lines that carry the same loop momentum and enter only through their sum are merged into one
                summed variable t (Int dx dy F(x + y) = Int dt t F(t)): x1 + x3 in g2_01 (either side of the
                self-energy) and x0 + x3 in g2_03 (either side of the external vertex, under the outer
                photon).  D = 4 for g2_00, g2_02; D = 3 for g2_01, g2_03.  This is what sincqed sums.

   Why "All" equals "Delta": the projective kernel is homogeneous of degree -6 in x0..x5 (and so is each
   1/V^n piece separately: T_n has degree n - 6), so with x = lambda xhat,
       Int d^6x S(x) = Int d^5xhat Sum_n T_n(xhat) Int dlambda lambda^(n-1) Exp[-lambda V(xhat)]/Gamma[n]
                     = Int d^5xhat pt(xhat),       pt = Sum_n T_n/V^n,
   and Cheng-Wu lets the slice be xhat4 = 1.

   Sinc sum: s_i = Exp[z_i], z_i = h k_i, k in Z^D;  ds_i = s_i dz_i, so a term is
       h^D Exp[Total[z]] f[Exp[z]].
   Values are coefficients of (alpha/pi)^2 before the mirror weight w (2 for g2_00, g2_01; 1 for g2_02, g2_03).
   The kernels pt<graph>[x0,...,x5, pw] are the generated C (kernels/g2/*.c, gen.py with FORM)
   translated by check/c2wl.py, with the power of V left open: pw[V, n] is 1/V^n (projective) or
   Exp[-V]/Gamma[n] (Schwinger).  Within each forest node they use the line identifications of the generator:
   the coefficients cp_i, cpp_i of the external momenta in line i, and the contraction weights w_ij, depend on
   the line only through its momentum routing, so they are computed once per class of equally routed lines.
   Lines with the same loop routing share w; lines whose routings differ only by the external momentum have
   cp_i = cp_r + const, e.g. at the external vertex cp_b = cp_a - 1, and only cp_r appears.  The Dirac algebra
   (FORM, O3 output) is done on these shared symbols, which keeps the kernels short.
   Line numbering (Volkov string "e; a-b, c-d", vertices 1..5 along the electron line): x0..x3 are the electron
   segments 1-2, 2-3, 3-4, 4-5; x4 is the first photon (a-b, always from vertex 1), fixed to 1 by Cheng-Wu; x5 is the
   second photon (c-d); the external photon sits at vertex e, so the pair split in u is (x0, x1) for e = 2 and
   (x1, x2) for e = 3.  Checked against the Symanzik polynomial U of each kernel.  Drawings: diagrams/.

   v2_00, the one-loop vertex with a Pi2 (electron loop) insertion on its photon, has its own kernel ptv200
   (hand-translated from kernels/g2/v2_00.c, generated by vp.py).  Its six Schwinger parameters, in the slots of
   the same x0..x5 interface: x0, x1 the electron lines either side of the external vertex (vertex 2 of the base
   graph "2; 1-3"); x2 and x4 the two photon pieces either side of the loop (x4 is fixed to 1 by Cheng-Wu in
   "Delta"); x3 and x5 the two lines of the loop.  In "DeltaGL" Cheng-Wu fixes the whole photon x2 + x4 = 1 instead,
   the pair (x0, x1) is split by the u rule, and sigma and the two loop lines are summed: D = 3. *)

(* ---------------- V-power rules ---------------- *)
projPW[v_, n_] := v^-n;
schwPW[v_, n_] := Exp[-v]/Gamma[n];

